-- title:
--   A cube is the product of open coordinate intervals
-- statement:
--   Work in Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$, and let $h > 0$ and $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$. Then the cube of side $h$ centred at $u$ is exactly the open coordinate box around $u$:
--   $$A_h(u) \;=\; \prod_{i=1}^{d} \left( u_i - \frac{h}{2},\; u_i + \frac{h}{2} \right),$$
--   where $u_i$ is the $i$-th coordinate of $u$ and the product is the set of points all of whose coordinates lie in the corresponding open interval.
--
--   The identity converts a cube, defined through the maximum norm, into a product set. That is the form in which the cube is amenable to coordinatewise reasoning and to the product structure of Lebesgue measure on $\mathbb{R}^d$, so it is the bridge between the geometric description of the tiles and any computation of their measure or of their intersections.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The right-hand side is literally a preimage under `WithLp.ofLp`, the map identifying `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)` with the plain function type `Fin d → ℝ`. This is only a change of type synonym, not a change of point set: the underlying set is the product `Set.pi Set.univ` of the open intervals. The intervals are open at both ends, so the cubes described here are the open cubes, not the half-closed ones.
